WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.540
INTRO:
Investment bankers make a lot of money.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:09.750
Straight out of college, these guys are usually
paid between $150,000 to $250,000 per year.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.950
And if you stick with it until your 40, you
can pretty reasonably expect to earn 7 figures

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.560
if not multi 7 figures.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.380
This makes investment banking one of the most
lucrative careers in the world and it’s

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.090
likely the most straight forward path if you
want to become stupid rich.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:28.870
The only job that might pay more is becoming
a hedge fund manager.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:34.219
In fact, the most successful investment bankers
in the world aren’t just deca or centi millionaires,

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:36.379
they’re actually billionaires.

00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:41.000
Jorge Paulo Lemann, for example, got into
investment banking in the early 1970s, and

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.710
today, he’s worth $20 billion.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:48.859
Similarly Joseph Safra got into investment
banking in the 1950s, and he was worth $22

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:50.820
billion at the time of his death.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.050
But, likely the most famous or should I say
infamous example of investment banking wealth

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.010
is the Rothschild family.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:01.079
The Rothschild family has been involved in
investment banking and other financial services

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:03.379
for over 200 years.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:08.570
And according to some reports, the entire
family is worth as much $500 billion today.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:14.610
So, clearly, investment bankers are killing
it, but what exactly do they even do and why

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.190
are they paid so much?

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:23.160
BOND FINANCING:
Starting of, one of the biggest roles of investment

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.660
bankers is to arrange financing for companies.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.620
This includes everyone from money burning
startups like Uber and WeWork to cash rich

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.600
companies like Apple and Microsoft.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.230
In fact, the larger cash rich companies are
often the more lucrative clients.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.330
But wait a minute, if they have so much cash,
why do they even need financing?

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:45.000
Well, I have a full video on how billionaires
and corporations use debt to their advantage

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.180
if you’re interested, but in essence, they
take out debt to benefit from inflation, reduce

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.940
taxes, and invest the difference.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.800
Apple, for example, currently holds $103 billion
in long term debt.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.090
Similarly, AT&amp;T currently holds $180 billion
in long term debt.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.210
But where does all this money come from?

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.409
Well, it’s not investment bankers themselves
per se, but investment bankers are the ones

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:10.799
that help these companies take on this debt.

00:02:10.799 --> 00:02:14.730
They usually leverage one of two strategies
depending on the company’s situation starting

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.060
with bond financing.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.909
If you’re not familiar with what a bond
is, it’s basically just a promise to pay

00:02:19.909 --> 00:02:23.299
back a certain amount of money with interest
by a certain date.

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:28.480
This Apple bond, for example, matures in 2027,
and in the meantime, Apple is promising to

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.010
pay a 3% coupon rate or basically a 3% interest
rate.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.290
One of the factors that make bonds attractive
for investors is their stability and reliability.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:41.209
I mean, what are the chances that someone
like Apple or Microsoft doesn’t pay back

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:42.209
their debt.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:45.920
This option is generally best for companies
that are already cash flowing well and can

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.389
comfortably pay back the debt.

00:02:48.389 --> 00:02:51.690
Investment bankers usually take care of the
back end of issuing a corporate bond.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.800
For example, they’ll plan out the bond issuance,
they’ll price it, they’ll complete the

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.709
required documentation, and they’ll market
the new bond to potential buyers.

00:02:59.709 --> 00:03:03.489
In other words, they take care of the legal
side of issuing bonds and ensure that the

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.700
desired amount is raised as efficiently and
timely as possible.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:13.560
EQUITY FINANCING
Issuing bonds is just one method to raise

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.560
money.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.120
Another popular method used by investment
bankers is equity based financing.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.040
This strategy is usually most common amongst
startups with little to no cash flow.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.769
Since they can’t afford to pay back debt
on a regular basis, they choose to dilute

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:28.620
their company and offer new stock to the public.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.230
Likely the biggest company that was dependent
on share offerings in recent history is Tesla.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.730
Given that they didn’t become profitable
till 2020, they were forced to constantly

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.749
offer new shares to raise money.

00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:42.810
Elon Musk often paid the investment banker
fees himself which literally cost him half

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.939
a billion dollars over the years.

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:49.000
This includes $213 million to Goldman Sachs,
$209 million to Morgan Stanley, and $85 million

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.180
to Bank of America.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.659
At first glance, this may seem like a ripoff.

00:03:53.659 --> 00:03:57.700
I mean how hard could it be to fill out a
prospectus and file it with the SEC so that

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.409
the the share offering can be completed.

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:04.420
Well, it’s not that hard, but a good investment
bank will offer much more than simply completing

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:05.420
legal paperwork.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.930
A good investment bank will ensure that it’s
well worth the clients money.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:13.169
Here’s the thing, an investment bank’s
goal with an equity offering is not just to

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:17.600
raise the desired amount of money but also
to minimize losses in stock value.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.060
For example, let’s say a $100 billion company
decides to raise $10 billion through a share

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.060
offering.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.319
In an ideal world, each share of the company
should drop by about 10% given that the total

00:04:27.319 --> 00:04:30.500
number of shares is being raised by approximately
10%.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.729
However, if the investment bank prices the
shares correctly and is able to get enough

00:04:34.729 --> 00:04:38.789
buyers on board, they may be able to limit
share price losses to 5%.

00:04:38.789 --> 00:04:44.610
For a $100 billion company, this means preserving
roughly $5 billion worth of shareholder value.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:49.470
Something else to consider is that you also
don’t want to leave any money on the table.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.820
If you’re able to raise $10 billion and
only sustain a 5% loss in share value, this

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.479
may be a sign that you could’ve raised even
more money.

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:00.050
You have to be careful though because trying
to raise too much money could lead to your

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.659
stock collapsing.

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:07.460
Fortunately, investment bankers are pros at
finding the sweet spot between raising money

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.590
and limiting shareholder losses.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.480
And from this perspective, paying a few hundred
million to a notable investment bank is well

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:17.380
worth it if it can help you preserve billions.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:21.650
UNDERWRITING:
Aside from providing these extremely valuable

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:26.180
financing services, investment banks also
often act as a sort of insurance company.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.789
You see, many of the companies that are looking
for financing already have plenty of issues

00:05:30.789 --> 00:05:32.139
to worry about.

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:36.180
And the last thing they want to deal with
is a financing deal going wrong.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:40.540
Fortunately, most investment bankers completely
eliminate this risk for the company by taking

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.360
on the risk themselves.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.690
Going back to the example of the $100 billion
company trying to raise $10 billion, a good

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:50.729
investment bank will give the company a percentage
of the $10 billion or the full $10 billion

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:52.030
upfront.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:55.300
And it will be up to the investment bank to
go out and complete the share offering and

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.439
recoup this amount.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:06:02.180
If the share offering goes well and the investment
bank is able to raise $10.2 or $10.3 billion,

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:05.449
they may be able to keep the excess money
depending on the contract.

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:09.849
On the other hand, if the share offering doesn’t
quite hit the mark and comes in at $9.7 or

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:14.569
$9.8 billion, the investment bank may have
to pay for the shortfall out of pocket.

00:06:14.569 --> 00:06:17.699
This doesn’t just apply to equity offerings
either.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:21.849
Investment banks generally provide a level
of protection for bond offerings as well.

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:26.010
Like an equity offering, with a bond offering,
the investment bank will usually pay the company

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:28.569
the full amount they want to raise upfront.

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:32.470
And it’ll once again be the investment bank’s
responsibility to actually go out and sell

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:33.570
these bonds.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.569
The price of bonds don’t fluctuate nearly
as much as stock though.

00:06:36.569 --> 00:06:39.950
Not to mention, underwriters usually sell
bonds for a set price.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:44.689
So, instead of trying to capitalize on market
fluctuations, with bonds, underwriters will

00:06:44.689 --> 00:06:45.689
sell the bonds with a markup.

00:06:45.689 --> 00:06:51.759
The standard underwriting fee seems to be
about $1 per bond or basically 1%.

00:06:51.759 --> 00:06:56.009
With smaller deals, investment banks will
just take on all the risk themselves, but

00:06:56.009 --> 00:07:00.130
with larger deals, they usually work with
other investment banks to spread out the risk.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:04.330
But either way, the client is usually able
to receive most if not all the money they’re

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:07.780
trying to raise upfront which is super helpful
when they’re trying to focus on running

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:09.349
a business.

00:07:09.349 --> 00:07:14.610
PRIVATE DEALS:
Moving onto the last major function of investment

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.689
banks, we have private deals.

00:07:16.689 --> 00:07:20.530
Oftentimes, companies don’t want to deal
with the public markets due to all the regulation

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:23.159
and public scrutiny that comes along with
them.

00:07:23.159 --> 00:07:27.389
Instead, they choose to seek out private financing
and cash outs through a method called private

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:28.389
placements.

00:07:28.389 --> 00:07:32.060
With this method, it’s the investment bank’s
responsibility to locate private buyers which

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.979
usually includes hedge funds and institutional
investors like insurance companies or retirement

00:07:35.979 --> 00:07:36.979
funds.

00:07:36.979 --> 00:07:40.389
Given that regulatory bodies aren’t that
involved in these deals, the primary purpose

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:45.539
of investment bankers within these deals aren’t
to fill out paperwork or ensure legal compliance.

00:07:45.539 --> 00:07:49.669
Rather, their primary purpose is to use their
street cred to attract private investors.

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:54.000
So, choosing a reputable banker is especially
important when it comes to successfully completing

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.260
a private deal.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:59.039
On top of finding private investors, investment
bankers are usually the core of setting up

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:00.819
mergers and acquisitions.

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:04.360
The investment bankers from either side of
the deal will get together and iron out the

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.930
details of the contract and decide on a fair
price.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:10.949
If you’re company is an acquisition target,
your investment bankers may be in talks with

00:08:10.949 --> 00:08:14.129
several potential buyers to hopefully get
you the best deal possible.

00:08:14.129 --> 00:08:19.629
So, that’s basically what an investment
bank does, but it’s not all sunshines and

00:08:19.629 --> 00:08:21.090
rainbows.

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:25.629
THE UGLY:
Given that investment banks aren’t super

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:30.479
entangled with the markets like hedge funds,
they’re usually not guilty of market manipulation.

00:08:30.479 --> 00:08:34.430
But, they do often engage in other questionable
activities.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.479
For example, one of the biggest concerns with
investment banks is potential conflicts of

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:39.479
interest.

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:43.070
You see, most investment banks aren’t just
investment banks.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:46.740
The vast majority of investment banks on Wall
Street also have securities research divisions

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:48.390
and trading divisions.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.190
Ideally, these different divisions aren’t
supposed to communicate with each other and

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:53.970
they’re supposed to act independently.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:58.460
But, in practice, we don’t really know how
strictly institutions follow these rules.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:02.389
There’s speculation that investment banking
divisions and securities reserach divisions

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:04.670
often work together to reel in customers.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:08.990
For example, the securities research division
might give Tesla a great price target and

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.200
rate the stock as a strong buy to suck up
to them and hopefully have real them in as

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.800
an investment banking client.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.820
This may not sound like a big deal, but try
telling that to the hundreds of thousands

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:21.520
of investors that may have been influenced
by their strong rating.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.209
Another potential conflict of interest is
any inside information that investment bankers

00:09:25.209 --> 00:09:26.760
may have access to.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.019
For example, let’s say Tesla wanted to complete
a share offering and approaches an investment

00:09:31.019 --> 00:09:32.019
bank.

00:09:32.019 --> 00:09:35.190
The investment bank could pass on this info
to the trading division of the firm which

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.470
would of course be insider trading.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.889
Aside from conflicts of interest, employees
and espeically low level analysts at investment

00:09:41.889 --> 00:09:43.650
banks are worked to the bone.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:48.500
For example, the average 1st year analyst
ends up working about 100 hours per week.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:53.790
So, that $150 to 250,000 salary that we were
talking about at the beginning of the video

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.660
is actually more like a $70-$80,000 salary.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.860
They’re basically just working an insane
amount of overtime.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:04.529
The hours do get better with experience, but
even at the highest levels, these guys are

00:10:04.529 --> 00:10:07.839
generally working 60 to 80 hours on a regular
basis.

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:12.490
And when they’re closing big deals, it’s
not uncommon for the VPs and directors themsleves

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.160
to work 100 hours per week.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.589
If this were a fast food restaurant or a retail
business, the media would be all over this.

00:10:18.589 --> 00:10:23.010
But, since these guys are paid a fat salary,
no one really bats an eye.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.340
INVESTMENT BANKING:
At the end of the day, investment banks are

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:32.010
basically the private bankers for large corporations
and governments.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.880
They help them raise massive amounts of money,
complete acquisitions, and they provide them

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.029
with a certain level of insurance.

00:10:38.029 --> 00:10:41.250
And considering the size of the money that
they’re dealing with, it’s not suprising

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:45.760
that they’re paid hundreds of millions if
not billions for each project they complete.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.600
Working this job isn’t easy though.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.200
There’s extraordinary amounts of stress
and work involved in raising said billions

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:53.300
of dollars and keeping clients happy.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:57.790
So, signing up for investment banking is definitely
a case of living to work.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:02.470
But, if you’re willing to make the sacrifice,
it’s no doubt one of the fastest ways to

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:03.920
get stupid rich.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:06.440
Would you guys consider becoming an investment
banker?

